version: 5
title: Explain the change without losing the reader
opening:
  title: Give each stop a reason to exist
  summary: >-
    A Bygone tour should explain what a change does and why its evidence matters. This branch
    addresses two sources of confusion: explanations that oscillate between sparse claims and scope
    dumps, and a viewer that can make a new topic look like a new comparison. First we’ll trace how
    purpose and comparison become explicit; then how navigation preserves the thread of the
    argument. Every scene here uses the same before-and-after revisions.
conclusion:
  title: Less repetition, more reasoning—and a visible way through
  summary: >-
    The branch gives authors an opening and conclusion, makes scene landings preview relevant
    evidence, and distinguishes a changed file from changed revisions. Back and Next traverse the
    explanation; constructed stages retain every code focus while telling their story once. Tests
    check comparison identity, navigation order, narration, and a cancellation failure exposed by
    switching code. Those checks establish mechanics, not that a reader understands the change. The
    remaining test is whether someone can explain the outcome and its cause after this tour.
range:
  base: 1e2e7b0392f205294fd98111409d3925d863b44e
  head: 2ddf51c6e968bb1e5bc827213eef00665a213488
anchors:
  causal-writing:
    file: skills/pr-tour-guide/SKILL.md
    revision: head
    contains: |-
      - Connect the observable outcome to its mechanism and supporting evidence in
        the main reading path. Preserve the causal links when shortening prose;
        “ignores layout events” is less useful than explaining why skipping scroll
        synchronization prevents a layout event from undoing a code reveal.
  bookends:
    file: src/tourFraming.ts
    revision: head
    contains: |-
      /** Authored bookends belong to a narrative, independently of its evidence scenes. */
      export interface TourPassage {
          title: string;
          summary: string;
          bullets?: string[];
      }

      export interface ChangeTourFraming {
          opening?: TourPassage;
          conclusion?: TourPassage;
      }
  reading-end:
    file: src/tourReading.ts
    revision: head
    contains: |2-
          if (tour.conclusion) items.push({ kind: 'conclusion', key: 'conclusion', sceneIndex: -1, stepIndex: 0 });
          return items;
  scene-preview:
    file: src/tourOrientation.ts
    revision: head
    contains: |-
      export function tourLanding(tour: ChangeTourManifest, item: TourReadingItem): TourLanding {
          if (item.kind === 'title' || item.kind === 'chapter' || item.kind === 'conclusion') return 'narrative';
          const scene = tour.scenes[item.sceneIndex];
          if (!scene || scene.kind === 'discussion') return 'narrative';
          if (item.kind === 'step' || scene.kind === 'text-diff') return 'evidence';
          return scene.overview ? 'overview' : 'preview';
      }
  actual-evidence:
    file: web/host.js
    revision: head
    contains: |2-
              // Panel selection describes rendered evidence even while the reader stays
              // on an intro, chapter, or conclusion with no evidence of its own.
              const evidenceItem = activePairIndex === undefined ? item : {
                  kind: 'step', sceneIndex: state.activeSceneIndex, stepIndex: state.activeStepIndex
              };
              const evidenceContext = evidenceItem && resolveTourEvidenceContext(state.tour, evidenceItem, state.zoom?.mode, activePairIndex);
              const context = rangeOnly || !evidenceContext ? resolveTourRangeEvidenceContext(state.tour, filePath) : evidenceContext;
              const displayedContext = { ...context, scope: filePath, scopeKey: JSON.stringify([filePath]), overview: false };
              const previous = lastReadingEvidence?.tour === state.tour ? lastReadingEvidence.context : null;
              const comparison = previous && (previous.comparisonKey !== displayedContext.comparisonKey || previous.scopeKey !== displayedContext.scopeKey)
                  ? describeTourTransition(previous, displayedContext) : describeTourComparison(displayedContext);
              // Remember the actual rendered pair, including user-selected panels and file exploration.
              lastReadingEvidence = { tour: state.tour, context: displayedContext };
  comparison-proof:
    file: test/tourFraming.test.js
    revision: head
    contains: |2-
          tour.scenes[0].steps[0].diff.path = 'other.ts';
          const scopeChange = resolveTourEvidenceContext(tour, intro);
          assert.match(describeTourTransition(initial, scopeChange), /Same revision comparison; file changed from app.ts to other.ts/);
          tour.range.baseRef = 'Different name';
          assert.equal(initial.comparisonKey, resolveTourEvidenceContext(tour, intro).comparisonKey);
          const reversed = clone(tour); [reversed.range.mergeBaseOid, reversed.range.headOid] = [tour.range.headOid, tour.range.mergeBaseOid];
          assert.notEqual(initial.comparisonKey, resolveTourEvidenceContext(reversed, intro).comparisonKey);
          tour.range.headOid = 'c'.repeat(40);
          const changed = resolveTourEvidenceContext(tour, intro);
          assert.notEqual(initial.comparisonKey, changed.comparisonKey, 'Same labels do not hide different OIDs');
          assert.match(describeTourTransition(initial, changed), /Revision comparison changed/);
  linear-navigation:
    file: web/host.js
    revision: head
    contains: |2-
          function showTourLinear(direction) {
              if (!isNarrativeMode()) return false;
              const target = getTourReadingTarget(readingItems, state.readingKey, direction, isImageOnlyTour(state.tour));
              if (!target) return false;
              activateReadingItem(target, false, true);
              return true;
          }
  navigation-proof:
    file: standalone/exportSmoke.js
    revision: head
    contains: |2-
                  await advance('tour-next', 'chapter:chapter');
                  await advance('tour-next', 'scene:scene');
                  await advance('tour-next', 'step:scene:step');
                  await advance('tour-next', 'conclusion');
                  assert.equal(await evaluate("document.querySelector('#tour-next').disabled"), true);
                  await advance('tour-back', 'step:scene:step');
                  await advance('tour-back', 'scene:scene');
                  await advance('tour-back', 'chapter:chapter');
                  await advance('tour-back', 'title');
  stage-once:
    file: src/changeTour.ts
    revision: head
    contains: |2-
                  title: focusIndex === 0
                      ? stage.title
                      : `${focus.file} · Evidence ${focusIndex + 1} of ${focuses.length}`,
                  // A stage is one argument supported by multiple code focuses.
                  // Keep its explanation once in the reading/narration sequence.
                  body: focusIndex === 0 ? stage.narration : '',
                  file: focus.file,
                  pairIndex: stageIndex,
  stage-recovery:
    file: web/host.js
    revision: head
    contains: |2-
                      if (scene.kind === 'deconstructed-diff' && !step.body) {
                          const firstStepIndex = scene.steps.findIndex(candidate => candidate.pairIndex === step.pairIndex);
                          if (firstStepIndex >= 0 && firstStepIndex < item.stepIndex) {
                              const explanation = readingItems.find(candidate => candidate.kind === 'step'
                                  && candidate.sceneIndex === item.sceneIndex && candidate.stepIndex === firstStepIndex);
                              const button = readingButton('Read stage explanation', `Read ${scene.steps[firstStepIndex].title}`, () => activateReadingItem(explanation));
                              button.className = 'tour-show-in-code tour-stage-explanation';
                              element.append(button);
                          }
                      }
  stage-proof:
    file: test/runTests.js
    revision: head
    contains: |2-
          const spoken = manifestScene.steps.flatMap((_, index) => narrationFor(index, 'continuous'));
          for (const stage of scene.stages) {
              assert.equal(spoken.filter(segment => segment.text === stage.narration).length, 1);
          }
          assert.equal(spoken.some(segment => segment.source.field === 'stage-title'), false);
          assert.equal(narrationFor(3, 'playback-start').find(segment => segment.source.field === 'stage-title').text,
              'Stage 2: Finish the behavior');
          assert.equal(narrationFor(3, 'playback-start').some(segment => segment.source.field === 'step-body'), false);
  highlight-cancel:
    file: media/script.js
    revision: head
    contains: |-
      function handlePendingWordHighlightCancellation(editor) {
          // Monaco 0.55's word highlighter ignores the promise from its cursor Delayer.
          // Model replacement and editor disposal cancel it. Handle only that pending
          // operation's expected cancellation, without hiding other renderer failures.
          const pending = editor.getContribution('editor.contrib.wordHighlighter')
              ?.wordHighlighter?.runDelayer?.completionPromise;
          void pending?.catch(error => {
              if (!isCancellationError(error)) throw error;
          });
      }
  highlight-proof:
    file: standalone/diffRegressionSmoke.js
    revision: head
    contains: |2-
              assert.deepEqual(await evaluate('window.highlightFailures'), ['Injected highlight failure'], 'Non-cancellation highlight failures remain observable');
              assert.equal(await evaluate(`${editors}.every(e => e.getOption(window.monaco.editor.EditorOption.occurrencesHighlight) !== 'off')`), true, 'Word highlighting stays enabled');
              assert.deepEqual(errors, []);
connections: []
chapters:
  - id: purpose-and-context
    title: Make the purpose and comparison clear
    scenes:
      - id: explanation
        title: What makes a tour an explanation?
        summary: >-
          Start with the author’s contract. The writing guidance asks for a causal account; the
          format gives that account a beginning and an ending. Notice which part requires judgment
          and which part the software can represent.
        steps:
          - id: causal-writing
            title: Keep the reason when cutting the words
            body: >-
              A terse label such as “ignores layout events” leaves the reader to infer why it
              matters. The revised skill asks authors to connect the outcome to the mechanism and
              its evidence in the main reading path. This is the strategy change: shorten repetition
              and inventory, while keeping the reasoning that makes a claim understandable.
            focus: causal-writing
          - id: bookends
            title: Separate the opening promise from the evidence
            body: >-
              Version 5 adds authored opening and conclusion passages, each with a title and
              summary. They belong to the narrative rather than a file or code step, so an author
              can establish the problem before presenting implementation details. These fields
              provide room for framing; their existence cannot guarantee useful prose.
            focus: bookends
          - id: reading-end
            title: Make the recap a destination
            body: >-
              The conclusion is appended to the continuous reading order after the scenes and their
              steps. That makes the ending reachable through the same navigation as the evidence,
              rather than burying the summary in the final code annotation. The navigation test
              later in this tour checks that readers can reach it and move back.
            focus: reading-end
        bullets: []
        tags: []
        takeaway: The format can create space for reasoning; the author must supply the causal argument.
      - id: comparison
        title: Does a new scene mean a new comparison?
        summary: >-
          With the purpose established, the next question is what the viewer puts beneath it. Follow
          the landing rule, the caption’s source of truth, and a test that distinguishes moving to
          another file from changing the revisions.
        steps:
          - id: scene-preview
            title: Show the next evidence unless an overview was authored
            body: >-
              The landing rule gives bookends and discussion passages a narrative view. A scene with
              code steps and no explicit overview gets a preview of its evidence; a step gets the
              evidence itself. A directory overview therefore becomes a deliberate authored choice,
              instead of the automatic visual interruption at every scene boundary.
            focus: scene-preview
          - id: actual-evidence
            title: Describe the comparison the reader actually sees
            body: >-
              The caption resolves the displayed panel pair and file, including manual panel
              selection and file exploration from a parent passage. It remembers that displayed
              context for the next transition. Otherwise a caption could describe the authored stop
              while the reader is looking at a different comparison—the precise ambiguity an
              orientation cue is meant to remove.
            focus: actual-evidence
          - id: comparison-proof
            title: A new file is not a new revision pair
            body: >-
              The test moves from app.ts to other.ts and expects “Same revision comparison.”
              Renaming a revision label leaves its identity unchanged; reversing endpoints or
              changing the head OID changes it. This checks that orientation follows the actual
              ordered revisions, rather than drawing conclusions from titles or file names.
            focus: comparison-proof
        bullets: []
        tags: []
        takeaway: >-
          Use a scene boundary to introduce a new question. Use resolved revisions to decide whether
          the comparison changed.
  - id: keep-the-thread
    title: Keep the argument intact as the reader moves
    scenes:
      - id: navigation
        title: Can the reader advance without steering the scroll?
        summary: >-
          Now keep that context while moving through the explanation. The buttons use the document’s
          reading order, and the exported-viewer test exercises both directions through its
          beginning and ending.
        steps:
          - id: linear-navigation
            title: Move to the next passage, not just the next code step
            body: >-
              Back and Next ask for the adjacent item in the reading order and activate it as linear
              navigation. This carries the reader through introductory passages, scene explanations,
              evidence, and the conclusion. Scroll remains available, but no longer has to be the
              main way to choose the next stop.
            focus: linear-navigation
          - id: navigation-proof
            title: The route reaches the conclusion and returns to the intro
            body: >-
              The export smoke clicks Next through chapter, scene, step, and conclusion; it then
              clicks Back through the reverse route to the title. It also checks that Next is
              disabled at the end. This is a browser interaction check for the navigation contract,
              not evidence that the prose is persuasive.
            focus: navigation-proof
        bullets: []
        tags: []
        takeaway: An ending only works as an ending if the reader can reach it through the normal controls.
      - id: multiple-focuses
        title: Why show several code focuses for one explanation?
        summary: >-
          A constructed stage is an authored intermediate version, rather than a Git commit. It can
          change several files or separated regions. Each focus should add evidence without
          restarting the argument. We’ll inspect how the compiler avoids repeated prose, how a
          reader can recover the explanation, and what narration tests establish.
        steps:
          - id: stage-once
            title: Explain the stage once while retaining every focus
            body: >-
              The compiler puts the stage title and narration on its first focus. Later focuses get
              a file-and-evidence title and an empty body, while keeping the stage’s comparison
              pair. The reader can inspect all of the code supporting one idea without encountering
              the same paragraph at every stop. This is behavior for constructed-stage tours; this
              tour itself stays on real revisions.
            focus: stage-once
          - id: stage-recovery
            title: Let a later focus lead back to its explanation
            body: >-
              An empty continuation body does not mean the reader must remember where the argument
              began. The viewer finds the first focus for the same comparison pair and adds “Read
              stage explanation.” This preserves a route back for someone who arrives at a later
              focus directly.
            focus: stage-recovery
          - id: stage-proof
            title: Continuous narration and direct entry need different context
            body: >-
              The test requires each stage’s narration to appear once during continuous playback.
              Starting directly at a later focus supplies “Stage 2: Finish the behavior,” without
              replaying the full body. That checks generated speech text: it does not establish
              actual audio quality or whether listeners follow the explanation.
            focus: stage-proof
        bullets: []
        tags: []
        takeaway: A stage is one argument, even when its evidence needs several stops.
      - id: switching-code
        title: Can switching evidence leave a stray failure behind?
        summary: >-
          Navigation also replaces editor content. The branch fixes one concrete failure found along
          that path: cancelling pending word highlighting could surface an unhandled rejection. The
          guard is narrow, and the regression check makes that boundary explicit.
        steps:
          - id: highlight-cancel
            title: Handle expected cancellation at editor teardown
            body: >-
              Before model replacement or editor disposal, the renderer attaches a handler to
              Monaco’s pending word-highlighting promise. It absorbs cancellation and rethrows other
              failures. This keeps expected teardown from surfacing as an error while preserving
              word highlighting. The access reaches into Monaco’s internal highlighter, so an editor
              upgrade needs to recheck this integration.
            focus: highlight-cancel
          - id: highlight-proof
            title: Keep unrelated failures observable
            body: >-
              The regression smoke replaces models with real highlighting work pending, then injects
              a non-cancellation failure. These assertions require that injected failure to remain
              observable, word highlighting to stay enabled, and the collected renderer errors to
              stay empty. The result is deliberately bounded: this guards one navigation-related
              failure, not every possible editor lifecycle error.
            focus: highlight-proof
        bullets: []
        tags: []
        takeaway: >-
          Stable navigation includes the editor work it interrupts; keep the cancellation boundary
          specific.
